WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:18.960
PROTONMAIL:
Have you ever heard of a service called ProtonMail?

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:25.380
It’s been out for nearly 10 years now, and
it’s extremely popular amongst enthusiasts.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.660
In fact, they have over 70 million users.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:34.630
But, the average person has never even heard
of the service.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:39.700
This is quite a shame because ProtonMail was
essentially designed to be a better version

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.850
of Gmail from every aspect.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.950
For example, ProtonMail doesn’t even know
who you are.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.710
They don’t even ask for a name to open a
ProtonMail email address.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:56.590
Conversely, Gmail asks for full name, date
of birth, gender, and phone number.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:01.440
The only information that you have to give
Protonmail is another email address or phone

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.950
number just so that they can verify that you’re
actually a human and that you’re not just

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.000
opening a crap ton of accounts.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.979
But, even this data is only briefly stored,
and is promptly deleted after verification

00:01:14.979 --> 00:01:15.979
is completed.

00:01:15.979 --> 00:01:19.390
The privacy doesn't just stop at signup either.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:24.090
Proton also doesn’t track your IP address
or review any of the emails that you send

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:25.370
or receive.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:30.929
Additionally, all of the emails that you send
and receive are completely end to end encrypted

00:01:30.929 --> 00:01:32.460
which is not the case with Gmail.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:39.320
Gmail does encrypt your emails when possible,
but their encryption is inherently inferior

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.689
because they rely on something called TLS
encryption.

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:47.530
The problem is that TLS encryption only works
when both the sending and receiving server

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.340
support the standard.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.270
We should also note that Google holds all
of the keys to said encryption.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:58.619
So, while TLS encryption may protect you against
hackers, it doesn’t actually protect your

00:01:58.619 --> 00:02:01.360
privacy against Google.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.550
ProtonMail on the other hand uses something
called PGP encryption which is not server

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:07.550
dependent.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:13.450
The only person who has the keys to open your
email is the actual recipients of your email.

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:18.970
So, objectively speaking ProtonMail blows
Gmail out of the water in terms of privacy

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:20.250
and security.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.100
Yet, Gmail still destroys ProtonMail when
it comes to marketshare.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:31.500
So, why does no one want ProtonMail despite
it offering everything that people supposedly

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:32.880
want.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.200
LACK OF RESOURCES:
Taking a look back, one of the main factors

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.700
that has held back ProtonMail over the years
is that they don’t have a very strong background.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.120
Virtually every popular email client that
has ever existed has been backed by a mega

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.250
tech company who’s main business is not
email.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:03:00.340
For example, Aol was Aol, Yahoo was Yahoo,
Hotmail was Microsoft, and Gmail was of course

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.480
Google.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.269
This is no coincidence.

00:03:03.269 --> 00:03:09.030
The reality is that creating an email service
is not only expensive, but it’s not exactly

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.120
lucrative either.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:15.060
I mean, have you ever wondered how Gmail even
makes money in the first place?

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:20.270
Historically, email providers made money by
showing you ads throughout the UI, but that’s

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.690
not really a thing in 2023.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:27.230
The way that email providers make money today
is by sending you personalized ads to your

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:28.569
inbox.

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:33.340
You know that promotions tab under your Gmail
account, that’s why that’s there.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:39.690
But, virtually no one even opens up that tab
or clicks on those emails, so most email users

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.550
don’t make the provider any money directly.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:47.200
But, companies like Google and Microsoft are
more than ok with that.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.940
Their goal with Gmail and Outlook aren’t
to make money.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:56.020
Their goal is to simply control marketshare
and create synergy between all of their services.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:59.900
It also makes their data collection and ad
targeting way easier.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:04.150
When you login into your Google account on
a new computer, they already know exactly

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:05.189
who you are.

00:04:05.189 --> 00:04:09.310
So, the next time you search on Google, they
don’t have to waste time serving you low

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:10.930
quality generic ads.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:16.720
Instead, they can serve you targetted ads
that have a far greater likelihood of converting.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:21.550
As you can see, email services are much more
of a customer acquisition avenue as opposed

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:23.560
to a real money driver.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:28.520
And this makes it extraordinarily difficult
for a company to be built around just doing

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.930
emails which ProtonMail has figured out first
hand.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:35.920
These struggles date back to the launch of
ProtonMail itself.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.910
Within just three days of beta launch, ProtonMail
was running into server capacity issues.

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:45.930
This forced them to temporarily suspend new
signups until they could expand server capacity.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:50.590
While understandable, this isn’t exactly
a great first impression.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:55.220
To make things worse, they had to resort to
crowdfunding to actually continue growing.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:05:00.330
Fortunately, their supporters were extremely
generous and helped them destroy their fundraising

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:03.039
goal, but PayPal had other plans.

00:05:03.039 --> 00:05:08.110
PayPal would end up freezing a quarter million
dollars worth of donations because they apparently

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:12.259
had concerns regarding the legality of encryption.

00:05:12.259 --> 00:05:17.220
Proton would eventually work through all of
these obstacles, but these are simply examples

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.760
of pain points that their competitors never
even had to deal with.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:25.460
Well, actually technically, Google did have
server capacity issues because they were trying

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.840
to offer 200 times more storage than everyone
else.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:32.960
But, they actually turned this into an advantage.

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:38.180
They maintained Gmail an an invite only platform
for many years which actually created an allure

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.180
of exclusivity.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:45.289
For Proton, however, these obstacles were
truly just obstacles.

00:05:45.289 --> 00:05:50.990
PAID SERVICE:
Aside from facing infrastructure challenges

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.970
that made it difficult to grow, Proton also
had to address the elephant in the room which

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:59.449
was how they were actually going to make money.

00:05:59.449 --> 00:06:03.650
Given that they weren’t collecting any user
data, they couldn’t create or deliver any

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.130
targetted ads.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:10.530
They could deliver generic ads but that would
kind of go against their whole brand image,

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.250
so that was off limits as well.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:17.750
This meant that the only way that Proton could
reliably make money was from their consumers

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:21.360
themselves which is not exactly a bad thing.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:26.259
The reason that consumers have to pay Proton
is because, with Proton, they’re actually

00:06:26.259 --> 00:06:27.919
the customers.

00:06:27.919 --> 00:06:33.169
With every other email service, the advertisers
are the customers and the users are actually

00:06:33.169 --> 00:06:34.169
the products.

00:06:34.169 --> 00:06:39.740
With that being said though, people have become
accustomed to getting email for free.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:43.620
So, Proton has to work through a pretty large
mental barrier.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:48.370
We should note that Proton does have a free
tier, but it’s not sufficient for any sort

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.160
of professional application.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:54.159
The free tier only comes with 500 MB of storage.

00:06:54.159 --> 00:06:58.270
If you’re regularly emailing documents,
presentations, and spreadsheets, you’ll

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:01.949
probably run of storage by the time you even
get to 100 emails.

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:07.350
You can boost this to 1 GB by completing a
few additional steps, but that doesn’t really

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.819
make the situation all that much better.

00:07:09.819 --> 00:07:15.030
And, I don’t think any of us want to go
back to the days of constantly deleting old

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.030
emails.

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:20.509
So, realistically, you would want to spring
for atleast the Proton Mail Plus plan which

00:07:20.509 --> 00:07:24.650
comes in at €5 per month or €48 per year.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:29.110
On the theme of protecting your privacy, we
should note that Proton accepts cryptocurrency

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:33.190
payments, so you don’t have to give them
your credit card details or anything like

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:34.190
that.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:38.520
Anyway, if you do spring for this plan, you’ll
actually get a service that’s comparable

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:39.720
to Gmail.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.810
You get an account that can support up to
10 email addresses, 15 GB of storage, custom

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:47.540
domains, and auto-reply features.

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:53.440
You also get access to the Proton Drive, Proton
Calender, and even the Proton VPN.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:59.720
If you spring for the Proton Unlimited plan,
you’ll have to pay €12 per month or €120

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:00.979
per year.

00:08:00.979 --> 00:08:06.819
But, this will boost your email address limit
to 15, your storage limit to 500 GB, and your

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.650
custom domain limit to 3.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:15.050
So, Proton is absolutely a viable email service,
but to unlock this functionality you have

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:16.050
to pay.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:21.150
And though people say that they value their
privacy and security over everything else,

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:27.039
the reality is that most people don’t value
those enough to pay for it, especially because

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:29.419
they’ve gotten used to not paying for such
services.

00:08:29.419 --> 00:08:35.660
We should also note that paying €50 or €120
per year for an email account is only viable

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.529
in the western world.

00:08:37.529 --> 00:08:41.800
And by the looks of it, Proton has already
won over much of this market.

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:46.540
So, until Proton is able to afford to give
out more generous free tiers, I suspect that

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:51.090
they’ll continue to struggle when it comes
to breaking into the mainstream market.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:56.470
FUNDAMENTAL SHORTFALL:
Even if we put aside Proton’s shortfalls

00:08:56.470 --> 00:09:01.850
in terms of infrastructure and revenue, they
still have to overcome likely the biggest

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:06.380
obstacle which is the obstacle of convenience.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:10.080
And I’m not talking about the convenience
of ProtonMail itself.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:16.760
They have a beautiful, modern, bubbly UI that’s
on par with if not better than the competiion,

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:20.670
so it’s not the convenience of the UI that
will hold them back.

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:24.860
Rather, it’s the convenience of email address
itself.

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:29.580
While people don’t like big companies collecting
personal data to make money, this data collection

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:33.740
is what allows for many of the conveniences
that we’ve become accustomed to as well.

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:35.800
Take login with Google, for example.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:39.930
The only reason that this service works is
because Google is able to pass on all of the

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:42.830
data that the service needs from your Google
account.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:48.519
But, Proton wouldn’t even be able to autofill
your name much less your phone number, address,

00:09:48.519 --> 00:09:50.650
or payment information.

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:54.430
Proton is trying to address this shortfall
with their recent acquisition of SimpleLogin.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:59.350
But, it’s not clear how useful this service
will be given Proton’s overall goals of

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:00.350
privacy.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:03.580
It’s not just new account creation that
will become more difficult either.

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:07.500
It’ll become substantially harder to sync
changes between devices.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:12.630
For example, let’s say you told Chrome to
save your password for Amazon.

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:17.760
With a Google account, the Chrome on your
iPhone and iPad would automatically know this

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:18.760
password.

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:23.520
And the next time you try to access Amazon
on your iPhone or iPad, it’ll autofill.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:29.090
Surely, that’s just a minor inconvenience,
but this could become a lot more annoying

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:32.310
when you get a new computer or laptop.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:36.310
Imagine how many chrome extensions you would
have to reinstall, how many passwords you

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:39.959
would have to reenter, and how many files
you would have to transfer over.

00:10:39.959 --> 00:10:45.540
Ironically, these were all givens when buying
a new device 10 to 15 years, but thanks to

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:51.100
the cloud, cookies, and yes data collection,
these have all becomes struggles of the past

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:52.550
for most of us.

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:56.329
By switching to ProtonMail, however, you would
lose many of these perks that come along with

00:10:56.329 --> 00:10:58.399
have a Gmail/Google account.

00:10:58.399 --> 00:11:03.889
But, atleast those are just inconveniences
as opposed to full on obstacles.

00:11:03.889 --> 00:11:08.670
The reality is that ProtonMail also comes
along with a gamebreaking obstacle.

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:14.680
Here’s the thing, what type of person would
be looking for a completely anonymous email

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:15.680
address.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:21.560
People looking for privacy yes, but also shady
people, spammers, hackers, etc.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:27.200
So, naturally, many companies and governments
are weary of Proton email addresses, and many

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.790
of them have actually full on blocked proton
email addresses altogether.

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:35.980
So, you’ll also need to have a backup email
address from one of the big providers to actually

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:40.389
use services from these companies, which kind
of defeats the whole purpose of Proton in

00:11:40.389 --> 00:11:42.150
the first place.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:47.170
You can get around this by using a custom
domain, but that gets into the whole paid

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:49.230
service problem all over again.

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:55.670
THE FUTURE OF PROTONMAIL:
In the end, Protonmail is a perfect example

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:58.670
of our love hate relationship with big tech.

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:02.899
You would think that the internet would be
jumping up and down at the sight of such a

00:12:02.899 --> 00:12:03.899
product.

00:12:03.899 --> 00:12:08.240
But, Protonmail has already been out for nearly
10 years, and they’re still only appealing

00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:09.970
to a niche demographic.

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:14.610
The reality is that while people hate that
their data is being collected, they’ve also

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:18.230
gotten used to all of the conveniences that
come along with that.

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:22.840
What Proton is trying to accomplish is truly
commendable, but while they’ve been able

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:27.730
to achieve a significant amount of success
within a niche market, their mainstream prospects

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:29.839
are not nearly as clear.

00:12:29.839 --> 00:12:34.170
It appears that the hurdles of getting people
to pay for an email service along with the

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:40.060
shortfalls in terms of convenience are simply
too much to overcome, but I’d be happy to

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:41.060
be proven wrong.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.649
Would you be willing to switch to ProtonMail?

00:12:43.649 --> 00:12:45.010
Comment that down below.

